            <!-- IPC Protocol -->
            <div slot="doc-4">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">IPC Protocol</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Communication between greeter and daemon uses JSON messages over Unix socket at /run/gardm.sock.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Requests (Greeter → Daemon)</h4>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20 mb-4">
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`// Create auth session for user
{"type": "create_session", "username": "alice"}

// Provide password
{"type": "authenticate", "response": "secret123"}

// Start user session
{
  "type": "start_session",
  "cmd": ["gar-session.sh"],
  "session_type": "x11",
  "env": []
}

// Cancel authentication
{"type": "cancel_session"}

// Power actions
{"type": "shutdown"}
{"type": "reboot"}
{"type": "suspend"}

// List available sessions
{"type": "list_sessions"}

// List available users
{"type": "list_users"}`}</code></pre>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Responses (Daemon → Greeter)</h4>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20">
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`// Success
{"type": "success"}

// PAM prompt
{"type": "auth_prompt", "prompt": "Password:", "echo": false}

// PAM info message
{"type": "auth_info", "message": "Welcome"}

// Auth failed
{"type": "auth_error", "message": "Incorrect password"}

// General error
{"type": "error", "message": "Session not found"}

// Sessions list
{
  "type": "sessions",
  "sessions": [
    {
      "id": "gar",
      "name": "gar",
      "comment": "Tiling window manager",
      "exec": "gar-session.sh",
      "session_type": "x11"
    }
  ]
}

// Users list
{
  "type": "users",
  "users": [
    {
      "name": "alice",
      "full_name": "Alice Smith",
      "home": "/home/alice",
      "avatar": "/home/alice/.face"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Session Management -->
            <div slot="doc-5">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Session Management</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardm supports both X11 and Wayland sessions with different startup procedures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- X11 Sessions -->
            <div slot="doc-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">X11 Sessions</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                X11 sessions reuse the X server started by gardmd.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Procedure</h4>
              <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gar-text-secondary space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li>X server keeps running (started at daemon init)</li>
                <li>gardmd forks child process</li>
                <li>Child authenticates with PAM and opens session</li>
                <li>Child drops privileges to target user (setuid/setgid)</li>
                <li>Child sets environment (DISPLAY, XAUTHORITY, XDG_SESSION_TYPE=x11)</li>
                <li>Child execve session command (e.g., gar-session.sh)</li>
                <li>Parent waits for child to exit</li>
                <li>When session ends, restart greeter</li>
              </ol>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Environment Variables</h4>
              <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/20">
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">Variable</th>
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">Example Value</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gar-text-secondary">
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">DISPLAY</td><td class="py-2 px-3">:0</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">XAUTHORITY</td><td class="py-2 px-3">/home/user/.Xauthority</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">XDG_SESSION_TYPE</td><td class="py-2 px-3">x11</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP</td><td class="py-2 px-3">gar</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP</td><td class="py-2 px-3">gar</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">XDG_VTNR</td><td class="py-2 px-3">1</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Wayland Sessions -->
            <div slot="doc-7">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Wayland Sessions</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Wayland sessions need direct VT access, so X server is stopped first.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Procedure</h4>
              <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gar-text-secondary space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li>gardmd stops X server (calls XServer::drop())</li>
                <li>Changes TTY ownership to target user (chown /dev/ttyN)</li>
                <li>Opens TTY as root and holds fd</li>
                <li>Forks child process</li>
                <li>Child calls setsid() to create new session leader</li>
                <li>Child authenticates with PAM and opens session</li>
                <li>Child drops privileges to target user</li>
                <li>Child sets controlling TTY (TIOCSCTTY ioctl)</li>
                <li>Child dups TTY fd to stdin/stdout/stderr</li>
                <li>Child sets environment (XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland, no DISPLAY)</li>
                <li>Child execve compositor (e.g., Hyprland, Sway)</li>
                <li>Parent waits for child to exit</li>
                <li>When session ends, restart X server and greeter</li>
              </ol>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Why Stop X Server?</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Wayland compositors need exclusive VT access and DRM master. Running X server blocks the VT. gardmd must stop X before starting the compositor, then restart X when returning to the greeter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Session Lifecycle -->
            <div slot="doc-8">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Session Lifecycle</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                The session lifecycle follows a fork/exec pattern with PAM session management.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Fork/Exec Flow</h4>
              <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gar-text-secondary font-mono mb-4">
                <div>Parent (gardmd, running as root):</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ fork()</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ wait for child</div>
                <div></div>
                <div>Child (still root):</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ setsid() [Wayland only]</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ PAM create client</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ PAM authenticate</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ PAM open_session (registers with logind)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ initgroups() (set supplementary groups)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ setgid(user_gid)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ setuid(user_uid)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ chdir(home)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ setup TTY [Wayland only]</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ execve(session_cmd)</div>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">PAM Session</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                PAM open_session is critical for systemd-logind registration. This creates /run/user/UID, sets up user bus, and grants device access (GPU, input devices). Without PAM session, Wayland compositors fail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Session Discovery -->
            <div slot="doc-9">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Session Discovery</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardmd discovers available sessions by parsing .desktop files from standard directories.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Search Directories</h4>
              <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gar-text-secondary">
                <div><strong>X11 sessions:</strong></div>
                <div class="ml-4 font-mono">/usr/share/xsessions</div>
                <div class="ml-4 font-mono">/usr/local/share/xsessions</div>
                <div class="mt-2"><strong>Wayland sessions:</strong></div>
                <div class="ml-4 font-mono">/usr/share/wayland-sessions</div>
                <div class="ml-4 font-mono">/usr/local/share/wayland-sessions</div>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3 mt-6">Desktop File Format</h4>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20">
                <div class="px-4 py-2 bg-gar-bg-secondary border-b border-gar-text-muted/20">
                  <span class="text-sm text-gar-text-secondary font-mono">/usr/share/xsessions/gar.desktop</span>
                </div>
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`[Desktop Entry]
Name=gar
Comment=Tiling window manager for X11
Exec=gar-session.sh
Type=Application`}</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Authentication -->
            <div slot="doc-10">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Authentication</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardm uses PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) for user authentication with a state machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- PAM Integration -->
            <div slot="doc-11">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">PAM Integration</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardmd uses the pam crate for authentication. The PAM service is "gardm" (/etc/pam.d/gardm).
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Two-Phase Authentication</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                PAM authentication happens twice:
              </p>
              <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                <div><strong>Phase 1:</strong> In gardmd (async, verify-only)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Creates PAM client with username/password</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Calls pam.authenticate()</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Returns success/failure to greeter</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Password stored temporarily for phase 2</div>
                <div></div>
                <div><strong>Phase 2:</strong> In child process (blocking, with session)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Creates PAM client with username/password</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Calls pam.authenticate()</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Calls pam.open_session() (registers with logind)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Keeps PAM client alive (std::mem::forget)</div>
                <div class="ml-4">→ Session closed when process exits</div>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Why Two Phases?</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Phase 1 verifies credentials quickly (async). Phase 2 happens in the child process after fork, so pam_systemd can correctly register the session with the child's PID as session leader.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Auth Flow -->
            <div slot="doc-12">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Auth Flow</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                The authentication flow is a state machine with three states.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Auth States</h4>
              <div class="space-y-3 mb-4">
                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-gar-bg-secondary/50 border border-gar-text-muted/10">
                  <div class="font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-1">Idle</div>
                  <div class="text-sm text-gar-text-secondary">No active authentication session</div>
                </div>
                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-gar-bg-secondary/50 border border-gar-text-muted/10">
                  <div class="font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-1">AwaitingPassword {'{ username }'}</div>
                  <div class="text-sm text-gar-text-secondary">Session created, waiting for password input</div>
                </div>
                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-gar-bg-secondary/50 border border-gar-text-muted/10">
                  <div class="font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-1">Authenticated {'{ username, password }'}</div>
                  <div class="text-sm text-gar-text-secondary">Credentials verified, ready for StartSession</div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Flow Diagram</h4>
              <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gar-text-secondary font-mono">
                <div>Greeter: CreateSession(username="alice")</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Idle → AwaitingPassword</div>
                <div>Daemon:  AuthPrompt("Password:", echo=false)</div>
                <div></div>
                <div>Greeter: Authenticate(response="secret123")</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Spawn PAM verify task (blocking thread)</div>
                <div>Daemon:  AwaitingPassword → Authenticated [on success]</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Success</div>
                <div></div>
                <div>Greeter: StartSession(cmd=["gar-session.sh"], type="x11")</div>
                <div>Daemon:  take_authenticated() → (username, password)</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Fork child, pass credentials</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Authenticated → Idle</div>
                <div>Daemon:  Success</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Security Features -->
            <div slot="doc-13">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Security Features</h3>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Failed Attempt Tracking</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Configurable lockout after N failed attempts (config: security.lockout_attempts). Greeter displays lockout timer and blocks authentication during cooldown.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Empty Password Blocking</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Can reject empty passwords via config (security.allow_empty_password = false).
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Privilege Separation</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Greeter runs as unprivileged process. Only gardmd runs as root. IPC provides privilege boundary.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Password Handling</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Passwords transmitted via IPC (local Unix socket only). Not stored on disk. Passed to child process via memory, used for PAM, then dropped.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- X Server Management -->
            <div slot="doc-14">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">X Server Management</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardmd spawns and manages Xorg server lifecycle.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Xorg Command</h4>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20 mb-4">
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`/usr/bin/Xorg :0 vt1 \\
  -keeptty \\
  -noreset \\
  -novtswitch \\
  -nolisten tcp \\
  -auth /dev/null`}</code></pre>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Flags Explained</h4>
              <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/20">
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">Flag</th>
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">Purpose</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gar-text-secondary">
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">-keeptty</td><td class="py-2 px-3">Keep using the VT (don't close TTY)</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">-noreset</td><td class="py-2 px-3">Don't reset when last client exits</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">-novtswitch</td><td class="py-2 px-3">Disable VT switching via Ctrl+Alt+Fn</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">-nolisten tcp</td><td class="py-2 px-3">No TCP connections (local only)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">-auth /dev/null</td><td class="py-2 px-3">No initial auth file (managed later)</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3 mt-6">Readiness Check</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                After spawning Xorg, gardmd polls for readiness using x11rb::connect() with 10-second timeout. Once connection succeeds, X server is considered ready for greeter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- VT Management -->
            <div slot="doc-15">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">VT Management</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Virtual Terminal allocation and switching via Linux ioctl syscalls.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">VT Allocation</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                gardmd finds an unused VT using VT_OPENQRY ioctl on /dev/tty0. Returns first available VT number (typically 1 or 7).
              </p>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20 mb-4">
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`fn find_unused_vt() -> Result<u32> {
    let console = OpenOptions::new().read(true).write(true).open("/dev/tty0")?;
    let mut vt: libc::c_int = 0;
    unsafe { libc::ioctl(console.as_raw_fd(), VT_OPENQRY, &mut vt) };
    Ok(vt as u32)
}`}</code></pre>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">VT Switching</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Uses VT_ACTIVATE and VT_WAITACTIVE ioctls to switch to the allocated VT.
              </p>
              <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-gar-text-muted/20 mb-4">
                <pre class="bg-gar-bg-primary p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gar-text-primary font-mono">{`fn switch_to_vt(vt: u32) -> Result<()> {
    let console = OpenOptions::new().read(true).write(true).open("/dev/tty0")?;
    let fd = console.as_raw_fd();
    unsafe {
        libc::ioctl(fd, VT_ACTIVATE, vt as libc::c_int);
        libc::ioctl(fd, VT_WAITACTIVE, vt as libc::c_int);
    }
    Ok(())
}`}</code></pre>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">TTY Ownership</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                For Wayland sessions, gardmd changes /dev/ttyN ownership to the target user with chown(). This allows the compositor to open the TTY for direct KMS/DRM access.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Power Management -->
            <div slot="doc-16">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gar-text-primary mb-4">Power Management</h3>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Power actions (shutdown, reboot, suspend) via systemd-logind D-Bus API.
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">D-Bus Methods</h4>
              <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/20">
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">Action</th>
                      <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-gar-text-primary font-medium">D-Bus Method</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gar-text-secondary">
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3">Shutdown</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">org.freedesktop.login1.Manager.PowerOff</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-gar-text-muted/10"><td class="py-2 px-3">Reboot</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">org.freedesktop.login1.Manager.Reboot</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2 px-3">Suspend</td><td class="py-2 px-3 font-mono">org.freedesktop.login1.Manager.Suspend</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Permission Check</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Can check if actions are allowed using CanPowerOff, CanReboot, CanSuspend methods. Returns "yes", "no", "challenge", or "na".
              </p>

              <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gar-text-primary mb-3">Interactive Flag</h4>
              <p class="text-gar-text-secondary mb-4">
                Power methods take interactive boolean parameter. gardmd uses false (no polkit prompt). Requires appropriate polkit policy for passwordless power actions at login screen.
              </p>
